{
  "id": "E02",
  "title": "3D cross-section graphs versus per-section 2D graphs",
  "question": "Do neighbours from adjacent serial sections improve spatial-domain recovery and expression estimation compared with 2D within-section neighbours, and how should z be weighted?",
  "data_origin": "real",
  "baseline_status": {},
  "notes": [
    "Part A tables were produced by an earlier invocation of this same script and reused (--resume-a).",
    "Part B raw table was produced by an earlier invocation of this same script and reused (--resume-b).",
    "DLPFC pixel-to-um conversion assumes the 100 um Visium spot pitch (137 px).",
    "GSE214349 z: section labels interpreted as positions in um (GEO: 20 um intervals, 10 um error); the previous order x 20 um reconstruction is reported as a sensitivity analysis.",
    "Part B tunes the smoothing weight on half of the genes and evaluates on the other half, so graphs are compared at their own best weight without using the evaluation genes.",
    "Part B includes degree-matched 2D controls because the cross-section graph has more neighbours per spot than the 6-neighbour 2D graph; 2D graphs with k = 6, 9, 10, 12, 15, 16, 18, 21, 22 and 24 bracket every cross-section graph's degree within about one neighbour. The 'degree-matched' table compares each cross-section graph with the 2D deviance-vs-degree curve linearly interpolated at the same mean degree (per block and seed; Wilcoxon over blocks).",
    "Interpretation: the 3D graphs have more neighbours per spot than the 6-neighbour 2D graph, so their effect must be read against the degree-matched 2D arms (Part A: 2D-joint-k9/k10; Part B: degree-matched table). Comparisons with the 6-neighbour 2D graph alone confound cross-section information with neighbourhood size.",
    "Part A degree-matched arms (2D-joint-k9, 2D-joint-k10) were added after the first full run showed the neighbourhood-size confound in Part B; they are reported alongside the original arms.",
    "Smoothing weight grid: 0-0.9 in steps of 0.1 plus 0.95 and 1.0 (1.0 = neighbour average only).",
    "MERFISH values are volume-normalised (not counts), so it is used only in Part C.",
    "Part B uses 3 count-splitting seeds per block; Part A uses 5 clustering seeds."
  ],
